    "gold_solution": "```python\nimport math\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"Power_Transmission_Network\")\n    m.setParam(\"TimeLimit\", 300)\n\n    # Problem Data\n    n_nodes = 5\n    nodes = list(range(n_nodes))\n    n_generators = 2\n\n    # Node coordinates (x, y) in km\n    node_coords = [(26, 39), (48, 4), (7, 79), (88, 54), (54, 56)]\n\n    # Node types\n    node_types = ['generator', 'generator', 'consumer', 'consumer', 'consumer']\n\n    # Power demands and generator capacities (MW)\n    power_demands = [0.0, 0.0, 41.0, 24.0, 23.0]\n    generator_capacities = [113.0, 163.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0]\n\n    # Wire physical properties (INDUCED PARAMETERS)\n    resistivity = 1.68e-08  # Ohm-meter\n    wire_area_mm2 = 500.0  # mm^2\n    wire_area_m2 = wire_area_mm2 * 1e-6  # Convert to m^2\n\n    # Operating parameters\n    operating_voltage = 220.0  # kV\n    line_capacity = 100.0  # MW\n    build_cost_per_km = 100000.0  # $/km\n    loss_cost_rate = 50.0  # $/MWh equivalent\n\n    # COMPUTE distance and resistance matrices (INDUCED COSTS)\n    # Using Ohm's Law: R = resistivity * length / area\n    def compute_distance_km(coord1, coord2):\n        return math.sqrt((coord1[0] - coord2[0])**2 + (coord1[1] - coord2[1])**2)\n\n    def compute_resistance(dist_km):\n        dist_m = dist_km * 1000  # Convert km to m\n        return resistivity * dist_m / wire_area_m2\n\n    distances = {}  # km\n    resistances = {}  # Ohm\n    for i in nodes:\n        for j in nodes:\n            if i != j:\n                dist = compute_distance_km(node_coords[i], node_coords[j])\n                distances[(i, j)] = dist\n                resistances[(i, j)] = compute_resistance(dist)\n\n    # Create edge list (undirected, i < j)\n    edges = [(i, j) for i in nodes for j in nodes if i < j]\n\n    # Decision Variables\n    # Build_i_j: Binary - whether to build line between nodes i and j\n    build = {}\n    for (i, j) in edges:\n        build[(i, j)] = m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f\"Build_{i}_{j}\")\n\n    # Flow_i_j: Continuous - power flow from node i to j (MW)\n    flow = {}\n    for i in nodes:\n        for j in nodes:\n            if i != j:\n                flow[(i, j)] = m.addVar(lb=0.0, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Flow_{i}_{j}\")\n\n    # Helper function to get build variable (handles i > j case)\n    def get_build(i, j):\n        if i < j:\n            return build[(i, j)]\n        else:\n            return build[(j, i)]\n\n    # Objective: Minimize construction cost + operational transmission cost\n    # Cost factor: converts resistance to cost coefficient\n    # Cost = LossRate * (R / V^2) * P^2\n    # factor = LossRate / V^2\n    loss_factor = loss_cost_rate / (operating_voltage ** 2)\n\n    # Construction costs (based on distance)\n    obj = quicksum(distances[(i, j)] * build_cost_per_km * build[(i, j)] for (i, j) in edges)\n\n    # Operational costs: Quadratic (I^2*R losses)\n    # Note: Gurobi supports quadratic objectives directly\n    obj += quicksum(\n        resistances[(i, j)] * loss_factor * flow[(i, j)] * flow[(i, j)]\n        for i in nodes for j in nodes if i != j\n    )\n    m.setObjective(obj, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # Flow conservation constraints\n    for node in nodes:\n        inflow = quicksum(flow[(other, node)] for other in nodes if other != node)\n        outflow = quicksum(flow[(node, other)] for other in nodes if other != node)\n        \n        if node_types[node] == 'generator':\n            # Generator: net outflow (generation) <= capacity\n            # Rewritten: inflow - outflow >= -capacity\n            m.addConstr(\n                inflow - outflow >= -generator_capacities[node],\n                name=f\"Gen_Cap_{node}\"\n            )\n        else:\n            # Consumer: net inflow >= demand\n            m.addConstr(\n                inflow - outflow >= power_demands[node],\n                name=f\"Demand_{node}\"\n            )\n\n    # Capacity constraints: Flow <= line_capacity * Build\n    for i in nodes:\n        for j in nodes:\n            if i != j:\n                m.addConstr(\n                    flow[(i, j)] <= line_capacity * get_build(i, j),\n                    name=f\"Cap_{i}_{j}\"\n                )\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "Subject: Planning memo \u2014 Transmission network design to connect generators and consumers\n\nWe are planning a cost-effective transmission network to move power from generating sites to customer loads across a compact service area. The candidate locations are provided as map coordinates in Node Coordinates (km):\n  - N0 (generator): (26, 39)\n  - N1 (generator): (48, 4)\n  - N2 (consumer): (7, 79)\n  - N3 (consumer): (88, 54)\n  - N4 (consumer): (54, 56), and the role of each location (generator or consumer) is identified in ['generator', 'generator', 'consumer', 'consumer', 'consumer']. The required power at each consumer is listed in Consumer demands: N2: 41.0 MW, N3: 24.0 MW, N4: 23.0 MW, and the maximum available output at each generator is specified in Generator capacities: N0: 113.0 MW, N1: 163.0 MW.\n\nDesign scope and decisions\n- We are free to propose new transmission lines between any pair of locations; there are no predefined corridors. Once a line is built, it can carry power in either direction.\n- Each built line has a thermal rating that limits how much power it can carry; the per-line rating is provided in 100.0 MW and applies to the magnitude of flow in both directions.\n- The capital cost to build a line scales with its length (based on the straight-line distance between node coordinates) at the unit rate in $100,000/km. This creates a direct incentive to keep the network as short as possible while still meeting performance needs.\n\nElectrical losses and operating cost\n- We will use copper conductors; the material property that governs ohmic resistance is specified in 1.68e-08 \u03a9\u00b7m (copper), and the conductor size is given by 500.0 mm\u00b2. For a given material and cross-section, resistance increases with length.\n- Resistive losses on a line arise from I^2R heating and follow a square law with respect to power flow. Because current is proportional to power divided by voltage, doubling the transmitted power approximately quadruples the heating on a given span. The operating voltage for the network is 220.0 kV; higher voltage reduces current and therefore reduces losses with a 1/voltage-squared effect.\n- We will monetize these losses using $50.0/MWh. In practical terms, the heating cost on a line is evaluated from the physical loss, which is proportional to resistance and the square of (power divided by voltage). This quadratic loss model is essential; we should not approximate losses linearly or price them by simply summing flows.\n\nPower delivery and planning assumptions\n- Power can only flow along lines that we actually build.\n- Each consumer must receive at least its required power, and no generator may exceed its maximum output.\n- For planning purposes, we will conserve power at each node: inflows plus local generation equal outflows plus local demand. Resistive losses are not deducted from these balances. Instead, they are accounted for entirely as a separate financial penalty through the heating cost described above.\n\nObjective\nOur goal is to identify which lines to build and how much power to route over them so that all consumers are served while respecting generator limits and per-line ratings, and to minimize the combined total of construction costs and quadratic operating (heating) costs. This design must reflect the physical reality that losses grow with the square of power flow and that operating at higher voltage is a primary lever to mitigate those losses.",
    "LLM_description_template": "Subject: Planning memo \u2014 Transmission network design to connect generators and consumers\n\nWe are planning a cost-effective transmission network to move power from generating sites to customer loads across a compact service area. The candidate locations are provided as map coordinates in {NODE_COORDINATES}, and the role of each location (generator or consumer) is identified in {NODE_TYPES}. The required power at each consumer is listed in {POWER_DEMANDS}, and the maximum available output at each generator is specified in {GENERATOR_CAPACITIES}.\n\nDesign scope and decisions\n- We are free to propose new transmission lines between any pair of locations; there are no predefined corridors. Once a line is built, it can carry power in either direction.\n- Each built line has a thermal rating that limits how much power it can carry; the per-line rating is provided in {LINE_CAPACITY} and applies to the magnitude of flow in both directions.\n- The capital cost to build a line scales with its length (based on the straight-line distance between node coordinates) at the unit rate in {BUILD_COST_PER_KM}. This creates a direct incentive to keep the network as short as possible while still meeting performance needs.\n\nElectrical losses and operating cost\n- We will use copper conductors; the material property that governs ohmic resistance is specified in {WIRE_RESISTIVITY}, and the conductor size is given by {WIRE_CROSS_SECTION}. For a given material and cross-section, resistance increases with length.\n- Resistive losses on a line arise from I^2R heating and follow a square law with respect to power flow. Because current is proportional to power divided by voltage, doubling the transmitted power approximately quadruples the heating on a given span. The operating voltage for the network is {OPERATING_VOLTAGE}; higher voltage reduces current and therefore reduces losses with a 1/voltage-squared effect.\n- We will monetize these losses using {LOSS_COST_RATE}. In practical terms, the heating cost on a line is evaluated from the physical loss, which is proportional to resistance and the square of (power divided by voltage). This quadratic loss model is essential; we should not approximate losses linearly or price them by simply summing flows.\n\nPower delivery and planning assumptions\n- Power can only flow along lines that we actually build.\n- Each consumer must receive at least its required power, and no generator may exceed its maximum output.\n- For planning purposes, we will conserve power at each node: inflows plus local generation equal outflows plus local demand. Resistive losses are not deducted from these balances. Instead, they are accounted for entirely as a separate financial penalty through the heating cost described above.\n\nObjective\nOur goal is to identify which lines to build and how much power to route over them so that all consumers are served while respecting generator limits and per-line ratings, and to minimize the combined total of construction costs and quadratic operating (heating) costs. This design must reflect the physical reality that losses grow with the square of power flow and that operating at higher voltage is a primary lever to mitigate those losses."
}